What is Logstash?
    Logstash is a server-side data processing pipeline.

Its job is to:
  Collect logs from different sources
  Parse logs
  Transform logs
  Filter unwanted data
  Enrich logs
  Send logs to one or more destinations

Unlike Fluent Bit, Logstash is not just a log forwarder.
Think of it as an ETL (Extract, Transform, Load) engine for logs.


Real-world example Your Java application generates this log:
    192.168.1.20 - - [31/Jul/2026:14:20:05 +0000] "GET /login HTTP/1.1" 404 1024

Elasticsearch stores it exactly like this if no processing occurs.
Searching becomes difficult.

Instead Logstash converts it into:
    {
  "client_ip":"192.168.1.20",
  "method":"GET",
  "url":"/login",
  "status":404,
  "bytes":1024,
  "timestamp":"2026-07-31T14:20:05"
   }

Now Kibana can easily answer:

Number of 404s
Top URLs
Top client IPs
Response codes
Requests per minute

This is why Logstash exists.


Logstash Architecture:
                INPUT
                  │
                  ▼
              FILTERS
                  │
                  ▼
               OUTPUT

Everything in Logstash revolves around these three blocks.

1. INPUT: This tells Logstash where logs come from.

Examples:

Application
      │
access.log (File)
      │
Logstash

Configuration:
input {
 file {
   path => "/logs/access.log"
 }
}

Beats: HTTP
input {
 tcp {
   port => 5000
 }
}



2. FILTER:

This is where Logstash becomes powerful.
Filters modify data before sending to Elasticsearch.
Most commonly used filters:

Grok Filter:
    Extract fields from plain text.

Example:
Input
GET /login 404

After Grok

method = GET
url = /login
status = 404


Mutate Filter:

Rename fields
  mutate {
     rename => { "host" => "hostname" }
  }

Remove fields
  mutate {
     remove_field => ["agent"]
  }
  
Convert datatype
  mutate {
     convert => {
      "status" => "integer"
     }
  }


Date Filter:  Convert timestamps.

date {
 match => ["timestamp","dd/MMM/yyyy:HH:mm:ss Z"]
}


GeoIP: Suppose log contains 8.8.8.8

GeoIP converts into

Country
City
Latitude
Longitude
Now Kibana can draw maps.


JSON Filter:

Input:
{"name":"Sai","age":23}

Output:
name = Sai
age = 23


Drop Filter: Ignore logs.

Example: Ignore health checks.

if "/health" in [message] {
 drop {}
}


3. OUTPUT:

Where should processed logs go?

Most commonly
Elasticsearch

output {
 elasticsearch {
   hosts => ["https://elasticsearch:9200"]
 }
}

Many outputs are supported:
    Kafka
    S3
    Redis
    File
    Email
    Many outputs are supported.


